T*
(I have been using the community fork of Maiao for a little while now, and it is excellent. For) Tj
T*
(anyone who prefers working with stacked diffs, but needs to interact with a more traditional) Tj
T*
(forge such as GitHub, try using Maiao for a bit. It just does the right thing in almost every) Tj
T*
(situation . It?s cut my frustration with GitHub at least in half, if not more. For the small) Tj
T*
(group of readers who are stoked about stacked diffs, that?s probably enough of a pitch. For the) Tj
T*
(rest of the world, let?s back up a little. On traditional forge platforms, pre-commit code) Tj
T*
(review happens on pull requests \(sometimes called merge requests \), where each pull request is) Tj
T*
(created from a git branch. Users that are new to git can then think of a branch as one) Tj
T*
(development effort toward a small goal. They keep adding commits to that branch as long as its) Tj
T*
(tip does not represent the goal state. At the end, when everything looks right and the change) Tj
T*
(is approved, they perform an explicit merge. This means they create a merge commit on the) Tj
T*
(upstream branch that references both the old upstream and the branch they worked on. \(Continue) Tj
T*
(reading the full article on the web.\)) Tj
